Z||000000||fieroburton||09-03-2001||02:12 AM||||is it good if you keep your fiero stored during wenter ?<BR> or is it good to drive al year long ?<BR>making a show fiero (just for shows and summer cruse) just wanted to know if its healthy for a fiero<BR>thanx   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>ps, can i put a 1986 computer in a 84 ?<p>[This message has been edited by fieroburton (edited 09-03-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||rogergarrison||09-03-2001||06:47 AM||||I dont believe in 'winter storage'. First your car dont know if its winter or not. Leaving a car unused for long periods is rough on it. Gas goes bad, seals all dry up, thing rust (even internally) thing like brakes sieze up, batteries die, list goes on. Some people have no problems, but I dont think its worth the risk. The only time I dont drive my car for an extended period is when theres snow and ice on the road. And thats more because Im afraid some idiot will run into me. Some people even are surprized I drive it in the rain, but I just tell them thats how i wash the bottom  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=11878523&p=52990885"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fierowrecker||09-03-2001||06:52 AM||||Hey Burton!<BR>I store my Fieros in the winter now...<BR>If you want to see what happens to a Fiero that does get driven in winter, my 84 is a good example...<BR>The drivers side floor is GONE!!!<BR>The only thing holding the seat in is the carpet!!!<BR>My 86's will not see a winter any more...<P>As to your computer question, yes... But change the prom if the numbers are different...<P>------------------<BR>crash... The Fierowrecker<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/uploads/crash_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fformula88||09-03-2001||10:14 AM||||It all depends on how long you want the car to last, and how bad the winters are.  Do they use a lot of road salt in the winter in Cincinnati?  Here in Buffalo, they use a ton... not a salt/sand mix like most areas but pure salt.  It has a habit of quickly eating through cars.  Including Fiero frames and floor pans!  My fathers last Fiero, an 87 coupe had huge holes in its floor pan too!  He gave it to my cousin this past spring, but the holes are going to be a pain to fix!<P>I don't exactly "store" my Formula in the winter, but I don't drive it much either.  Usually I can get it out a couple times a month except in January, and sometimes February around here.  I don't drive it if salt will get on the frame.  It has very little rust on it now and I am keeping it that way.  It runs a little rough after it has sat for a couple weeks, but by the time it warms up its fine. <P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierosig2.JPG"> <BR>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000004||skitime||09-03-2001||11:24 AM||||At the age of the Fieros road salt is especially dangerous. Add constantly damp rain/snow weather and you have prime conditions for what is in the pictures.  (Friends car in Canada) If you are lucky enough to pick and choose the days to drive in the winter then there is no harm and even does some good.  I think a Fiero that is a daily driver all winter  in the snowbelt is doomed eventually.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P8120021.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P8120019.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Master_Sushi||09-03-2001||05:18 PM||||I try not to drive my fiero in the winter to much. I do have snow tires mounted on my orignal tech wheels. So when I do have to go out, I don't have traction probs. But when i do go out... fieros have to be the funest car in the snow ever  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. Sometime after a few inches of fresh snow I just go out for a few hours and do massive donuts/spins  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif"> in empty parking lots.<P>I don't have any rust on my coupe right now. I had a tiny bit on the batt tray, but I promptly treated it. I spray the salt off the bottom of my car every few days during the winter.<P>------------------<BR>88 Fiero Coupe...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mesopotamis_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||09-03-2001||07:14 PM||||My Formula with just under 180K miles has been a daily driver in SW Ohio for its entire life since leaving the lot. One trunk corner was getting a a little brown spot, so I removed the fender curtains, shot everything with rust killer, got it all cleaned up good, and sprayed the entire wheel wells with rubberized underbody coating stuff. The rest of the frame is still vulnerable, but for 180K miles and seeing very nasty winters since '88, it is still rust free aside from the brown spot I mentioned.<P>My '86SE has a spot about the size of a quarter, I need to get to it sometime and do the same.||||reg||1
